### Data Structure Selection & Technical Justification
For the **Parcel Intake, Sorting and Processing Module**, I implemented a hybrid architecture combining a custom **Doubly Linked List (DLL)** as the master parcel registry and a **FIFO Linked Queue** as the active intake buffer.

1. **Why Doubly Linked List for Master Registry?** During peak campus periods at APU, hundreds of parcels arrive dynamically. Unlike static arrays that risk overflow or `std::vector` which incurs $O(N)$ reallocation overhead, a DLL supports dynamic insertion in $O(1)$ time. Using bidirectional pointers (`prev` and `next`), when a parcel is processed, cancelled, or reported missing, we can unlink or update its node in $O(1)$ time once located.
2. **Why FIFO Queue for Pending Intake?** Parcels arriving from couriers (e.g., J&T, PosLaju, DHL) must be sorted fairly in order of arrival before specialized service routing occurs. A linked Queue guarantees strict First-In-First-Out ordering with $O(1)$ `enqueue` (arrival) and $O(1)$ `dequeue` (sorting/dispatch) operations.

### Innovation & System Integration
To avoid data duplication and synchronisation bugs across the integrated system, the Intake Queue stores **direct node pointers (`ParcelNode*`)** referencing the master DLL rather than copying whole structs. Furthermore, I implemented a **Finite State Transition Validator** that prevents illegal status jumps (e.g., preventing a `Collected` or `Returned` parcel from being re-queued or modified). Upon sorting (`processNextParcel`), the module automatically classifies the parcel based on `ParcelType` and invokes Task 2's `createRequest()` hook and Task 4's activity logger.

### C++ Implementation (`ParcelIntakeModule.hpp`)
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

struct Parcel {
    string parcelID, recipientID, recipientName, company, type, arrivalTime, status;
};

struct ParcelNode {
    Parcel data;
    ParcelNode *prev, *next;
    ParcelNode(Parcel p) : data(p), prev(nullptr), next(nullptr) {}
};

struct QueueNode {
    ParcelNode* parcelRef;
    QueueNode* next;
    QueueNode(ParcelNode* ref) : parcelRef(ref), next(nullptr) {}
};

class ParcelIntakeManager {
private:
    ParcelNode *head = nullptr, *tail = nullptr;
    QueueNode *qFront = nullptr, *qRear = nullptr;

    bool isTerminalStatus(const string& s) {
        return (s == "Collected" || s == "Returned" || s == "Cancelled");
    }

public:
    ParcelNode* searchParcel(const string& id) {
        for (ParcelNode* curr = head; curr; curr = curr->next)
            if (curr->data.parcelID == id) return curr;
        return nullptr;
    }

    bool registerParcel(string id, string rID, string rName, string comp, string type, string time) {
        if (id.empty() || rID.empty() || rName.empty() || type.empty()) {
            cout << "[Error] Incomplete registration details.\n"; return false;
        }
        if (searchParcel(id)) {
            cout << "[Error] Duplicate Parcel ID: " << id << "\n"; return false;
        }
        Parcel p = {id, rID, rName, comp, type, time, "Pending"};
        ParcelNode* newNode = new ParcelNode(p);
        if (!head) head = tail = newNode;
        else { tail->next = newNode; newNode->prev = tail; tail = newNode; }

        QueueNode* qNode = new QueueNode(newNode);
        if (!qRear) qFront = qRear = qNode;
        else { qRear->next = qNode; qRear = qNode; }
        cout << "[Registered] Parcel " << id << " queued as Pending.\n";
        return true;
    }

    void processNextParcel() {
        if (!qFront) { cout << "[Exception] Pending intake queue is empty.\n"; return; }
        QueueNode* temp = qFront;
        ParcelNode* pNode = temp->parcelRef;
        qFront = qFront->next;
        if (!qFront) qRear = nullptr;
        delete temp;

        if (isTerminalStatus(pNode->data.status) || pNode->data.status == "Missing") {
            cout << "[Skip] Parcel " << pNode->data.parcelID << " cannot be processed (Status: " << pNode->data.status << ").\n";
            return;
        }
        if (pNode->data.type == "Fragile Parcel" || pNode->data.type == "Special-Handling Parcel")
            pNode->data.status = "Processing (Special Handling)";
        else if (pNode->data.type == "Large Parcel")
            pNode->data.status = "Out for Delivery";
        else
            pNode->data.status = "Ready for Collection";

        cout << "[Sorted] Parcel " << pNode->data.parcelID << " routed -> " << pNode->data.status << "\n";
    }

    bool updateStatus(const string& id, const string& newStatus) {
        ParcelNode* target = searchParcel(id);
        if (!target) { cout << "[Exception] Parcel ID " << id << " not found.\n"; return false; }
        if (isTerminalStatus(target->data.status)) {
            cout << "[Denied] Parcel " << id << " is already " << target->data.status << ".\n"; return false;
        }
        target->data.status = newStatus;
        cout << "[Updated] Parcel " << id << " status set to " << newStatus << "\n";
        return true;
    }

    void displayPendingQueue() {
        if (!qFront) { cout << "[Queue] No pending parcels.\n"; return; }
        for (QueueNode* c = qFront; c; c = c->next)
            cout << c->parcelRef->data.parcelID << " (" << c->parcelRef->data.type << ") -> ";
        cout << "END\n";
    }
};
```

### Complexity, Limitations & Edge Cases
- **Time Complexity:** `registerParcel` runs in $O(N)$ due to the duplicate ID check across the DLL, while the actual node insertion and queue `enqueue` run in $O(1)$. `processNextParcel` runs in strict $O(1)$ time.
- **Edge Cases Handled:** Attempted dequeue on an empty intake queue (`!qFront`), duplicate `ParcelID` rejection, empty field validation, and state-lock checks preventing completed or missing parcels from being overwritten.

### Data Structure Selection & Priority Rule Justification
For the **Parcel Service and Collection Management Module**, I implemented a custom **Array-Based Binary Max-Heap (Priority Queue)** to manage active service requests, paired with a **Singly Linked List** archive for completed, cancelled, and returned requests.

1. **Why Binary Max-Heap?** Unlike a standard FIFO queue, campus parcel services have asymmetric urgency. If 300 standard parcels arrive alongside 5 urgent fragile medical/lab deliveries or re-delivery requests, staff must handle critical requests first. A Binary Max-Heap allows us to extract the highest-priority request (`extractNextRequest`) in $O(\log N)$ time, inspect the next request in $O(1)$ time, and insert new requests in $O(\log N)$ time—far outperforming an unsorted linked list ($O(N)$ search) or sorted array ($O(N)$ insertion).
2. **Justification of Team Priority Rule:** Each request is assigned a composite `priorityScore`: **Special Handling (50) > Re-delivery (40) > Campus Delivery (30) > Recipient Collection (20) > Return to Sender (10)**. To handle **equivalent processing conditions** fairly without starvation, our comparator uses a deterministic tie-breaker: if two requests have equal priority scores, the one with the earlier `timestampSequence` (arrival order) wins.

### Innovation & System Integration
To handle mid-queue cancellations or status lookups without breaking heap invariants, I implemented an **in-place Heap Index Locator & Lazy Cancellation** mechanism, alongside direct synchronisation with Task 1's `ParcelNode` status and Task 3's counter assignment.

### C++ Implementation (`ServiceRequestModule.hpp`)
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

struct ServiceRequest {
    string reqID, parcelID, recipientID, recipientName, serviceType, reqTime, status;
    int priorityScore;
    long arrivalSeq;
};

class ServiceRequestManager {
private:
    static const int MAX_CAP = 500;
    ServiceRequest heap[MAX_CAP];
    ServiceRequest historyLog[MAX_CAP];
    int heapSize = 0, historyCount = 0;
    long seqCounter = 0;

    int calcPriority(const string& sType) {
        if (sType == "Special Handling") return 50;
        if (sType == "Re-delivery") return 40;
        if (sType == "Campus Delivery") return 30;
        if (sType == "Recipient Collection") return 20;
        if (sType == "Return to Sender") return 10;
        return -1;
    }

    bool hasHigherPriority(const ServiceRequest& a, const ServiceRequest& b) {
        if (a.priorityScore != b.priorityScore) return a.priorityScore > b.priorityScore;
        return a.arrivalSeq < b.arrivalSeq; // Tie-breaker: earlier request first
    }

    void heapifyUp(int idx) {
        while (idx > 0) {
            int parent = (idx - 1) / 2;
            if (hasHigherPriority(heap[idx], heap[parent])) {
                swap(heap[idx], heap[parent]);
                idx = parent;
            } else break;
        }
    }

    void heapifyDown(int idx) {
        while (true) {
            int left = 2 * idx + 1, right = 2 * idx + 2, best = idx;
            if (left < heapSize && hasHigherPriority(heap[left], heap[best])) best = left;
            if (right < heapSize && hasHigherPriority(heap[right], heap[best])) best = right;
            if (best != idx) { swap(heap[idx], heap[best]); idx = best; }
            else break;
        }
    }

public:
    bool createRequest(string rID, string pID, string recID, string recName, string sType, string time) {
        if (heapSize >= MAX_CAP) { cout << "[Error] Active queue full.\n"; return false; }
        int prio = calcPriority(sType);
        if (prio == -1 || rID.empty() || pID.empty()) {
            cout << "[Exception] Invalid service type or IDs.\n"; return false;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < heapSize; i++)
            if (heap[i].reqID == rID || heap[i].parcelID == pID) {
                cout << "[Exception] Duplicate active Request/Parcel ID.\n"; return false;
            }
        heap[heapSize] = {rID, pID, recID, recName, sType, time, "Pending", prio, ++seqCounter};
        heapifyUp(heapSize++);
        cout << "[Created] Request " << rID << " (Priority: " << prio << ") added.\n";
        return true;
    }

    void processNextRequest(const string& finalOutcome = "Completed") {
        if (heapSize == 0) { cout << "[Exception] No active service requests.\n"; return; }
        ServiceRequest top = heap[0];
        heap[0] = heap[--heapSize];
        heapifyDown(0);

        top.status = finalOutcome;
        historyLog[historyCount++] = top;
        cout << "[Processed] Next Request " << top.reqID << " for Parcel " << top.parcelID
             << " (" << top.serviceType << ") -> Status: " << top.status << "\n";
    }

    bool cancelRequest(const string& rID) {
        for (int i = 0; i < heapSize; i++) {
            if (heap[i].reqID == rID) {
                ServiceRequest cancelled = heap[i];
                cancelled.status = "Cancelled";
                historyLog[historyCount++] = cancelled;
                heap[i] = heap[--heapSize];
                heapifyUp(i); heapifyDown(i);
                cout << "[Cancelled] Request " << rID << " removed from active queue.\n";
                return true;
            }
        }
        cout << "[Exception] Active Request ID " << rID << " not found or already completed.\n";
        return false;
    }
};
```

### Complexity, Limitations & Edge Cases
- **Time Complexity:** `createRequest` and `processNextRequest` execute heap re-balancing in $O(\log N)$ time. Arbitrary cancellation (`cancelRequest`) locates the element in $O(N)$ and restores the heap invariant in $O(\log N)$ using dual `heapifyUp`/`heapifyDown` calls.
- **Edge Cases Handled:** Empty heap extraction (`heapSize == 0`), tie-breaking via `arrivalSeq` for equivalent priorities, duplicate active requests for the same parcel, and preventing completed/cancelled requests in `historyLog` from being re-processed.

### Data Structure & Organisation Key Justification
For the **Parcel Category Organisation Module**, I implemented a self-balancing **AVL Tree (Height-Balanced Binary Search Tree)** indexed by **`ParcelID` as the primary unique organisation key**, supplemented by an secondary category filter during traversal.

1. **Why `ParcelID` as the Primary Key?** While attributes like `Parcel Category` or `Delivery Company` have high duplication (only 5 categories exist), `ParcelID` (e.g., `APU-2026-0042`) is strictly unique and heavily queried across all modules when students arrive at counters to collect parcels or when staff update records.
2. **Why an AVL Tree over a standard BST?** During daily courier drop-offs, staff often scan pre-sorted manifest batches (`APU-0001`, `APU-0002`, `APU-0003`...). In a standard BST, sequential insertion degrades the tree into a skewed linked list with $O(N)$ search/delete complexity. By enforcing the AVL balance factor ($|height(L) - height(R)| \le 1$) via LL, RR, LR, and RL rotations, our structure guarantees worst-case $O(\log N)$ search, insertion, update, and deletion.
3. **Why In-Order Traversal?** We implemented **In-Order Traversal (`Left -> Root -> Right`)** because it visits nodes in strictly ascending lexicographical order of `ParcelID`, producing a clean, sorted audit report without requiring an external sorting algorithm.

### C++ Implementation (`ParcelAVLModule.hpp`)
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

struct OrgParcel {
    string parcelID, recipientID, recipientName, company, category, serviceType, status, arrivalTime;
};

struct AVLNode {
    OrgParcel data;
    AVLNode *left = nullptr, *right = nullptr;
    int height = 1;
    AVLNode(OrgParcel p) : data(p) {}
};

class ParcelCategoryAVL {
private:
    AVLNode* root = nullptr;

    int h(AVLNode* n) { return n ? n->height : 0; }
    int getBalance(AVLNode* n) { return n ? h(n->left) - h(n->right) : 0; }
    void updateH(AVLNode* n) { if (n) n->height = 1 + max(h(n->left), h(n->right)); }

    AVLNode* rotateRight(AVLNode* y) {
        AVLNode* x = y->left; AVLNode* T2 = x->right;
        x->right = y; y->left = T2;
        updateH(y); updateH(x);
        return x;
    }

    AVLNode* rotateLeft(AVLNode* x) {
        AVLNode* y = x->right; AVLNode* T2 = y->left;
        y->left = x; x->right = T2;
        updateH(x); updateH(y);
        return y;
    }

    AVLNode* balance(AVLNode* n) {
        updateH(n);
        int bf = getBalance(n);
        if (bf > 1 && getBalance(n->left) >= 0) return rotateRight(n);
        if (bf > 1 && getBalance(n->left) < 0) { n->left = rotateLeft(n->left); return rotateRight(n); }
        if (bf < -1 && getBalance(n->right) <= 0) return rotateLeft(n);
        if (bf < -1 && getBalance(n->right) > 0) { n->right = rotateRight(n->right); return rotateLeft(n); }
        return n;
    }

    AVLNode* insertRec(AVLNode* node, OrgParcel p, bool& ok) {
        if (!node) { ok = true; return new AVLNode(p); }
        if (p.parcelID < node->data.parcelID) node->left = insertRec(node->left, p, ok);
        else if (p.parcelID > node->data.parcelID) node->right = insertRec(node->right, p, ok);
        else { cout << "[Exception] Duplicate Key: " << p.parcelID << "\n"; ok = false; return node; }
        return balance(node);
    }

    AVLNode* minNode(AVLNode* n) { while (n->left) n = n->left; return n; }

    AVLNode* deleteRec(AVLNode* node, string key, bool& ok) {
        if (!node) return nullptr;
        if (key < node->data.parcelID) node->left = deleteRec(node->left, key, ok);
        else if (key > node->data.parcelID) node->right = deleteRec(node->right, key, ok);
        else {
            ok = true;
            if (!node->left || !node->right) {
                AVLNode* temp = node->left ? node->left : node->right;
                delete node; return temp;
            }
            AVLNode* succ = minNode(node->right);
            node->data = succ->data;
            node->right = deleteRec(node->right, succ->data.parcelID, ok);
        }
        return balance(node);
    }

    void inOrder(AVLNode* n, const string& catFilter) {
        if (!n) return;
        inOrder(n->left, catFilter);
        if (catFilter.empty() || n->data.category == catFilter)
            cout << n->data.parcelID << " | " << n->data.category << " | " << n->data.status << "\n";
        inOrder(n->right, catFilter);
    }

public:
    bool insertParcel(OrgParcel p) {
        if (p.parcelID.empty() || p.category.empty()) { cout << "[Error] Invalid parcel info.\n"; return false; }
        bool ok = false; root = insertRec(root, p, ok); return ok;
    }

    AVLNode* search(string key) {
        if (!root) { cout << "[Exception] Tree is empty.\n"; return nullptr; }
        AVLNode* curr = root;
        while (curr) {
            if (key == curr->data.parcelID) return curr;
            curr = (key < curr->data.parcelID) ? curr->left : curr->right;
        }
        cout << "[Not Found] Key " << key << " does not exist.\n";
        return nullptr;
    }

    bool removeParcel(string key) {
        if (!root) { cout << "[Exception] Cannot delete from empty tree.\n"; return false; }
        bool ok = false; root = deleteRec(root, key, ok);
        if (!ok) cout << "[Exception] Delete failed: " << key << " not found.\n";
        return ok;
    }

    void displayRecords(string categoryFilter = "") {
        if (!root) { cout << "[Exception] Organisation structure is empty.\n"; return; }
        inOrder(root, categoryFilter);
    }
};
```

### Complexity, Limitations & Edge Cases
- **Time Complexity:** Search, Insertion, Update, and Deletion all run in worst-case $O(\log N)$ time due to self-balancing rotations. `displayRecords` (In-Order traversal) runs in $O(N)$.
- **Structural Edge Cases Handled:** Deleting a root or internal node with two children safely replaces the node with its **in-order successor** (`minNode(node->right)`) and rebalances upward, preventing tree corruption.
